# The annotations of tape/basic (kit/mkgame.py --part src/tape/basic.json). Format: ../../../../docs/ann-format.md.
#
# TZX block 1, the BASIC program "DEFLEKTOR " (LINE 0, 384 bytes) at PROG $5CCB: line 0 ($5CCB-$5CFE, the program's
# 52 bytes), six bytes of its "variables", the code at $5D05 that USR enters, and the loader's first stage
# $5D17-$5E4A, which that code copies to $B626-$B759 and runs there. Witness: c1 (power-on, the original TZX).
# "Witness: L1 0 (c1)" lines are placeholders the lead's --fix-witness fills. Evidence: work/ann/04-loader/
# (hooks.py, reader.py: tools/tapesim.py's run of the original turbo block, instrumented) and work/ann/04-loader.md.

@equ $3D00 RomFont 768 ; the 48K ROM's character set, characters 32-127, 8 bytes each
@equ $5800 Attributes 768 ; the screen attributes
@equ $B75A Stage2 ; the loader's second stage (src/loader): the first load, and the hook's work at $B75A
@equ $B856 LoadRest ; stage 2's entry: the load of its last two pieces (src/loader)

@module basic ; The BASIC: line 0, whose USR enters the code after the program
@about The program is one line, numbered 0 and auto-run: RANDOMIZE USR (PEEK 23641+256*PEEK 23642+4) as LIST shows it, whose hidden five-byte numbers are 23635 (PROG), 23636 and 58: USR (PROG + 58) = $5D05. The digits are a decoy: 23641/23642 is E_LINE, so a reader of the listing would look for the code at E_LINE + 4.
@about The line has no ENTER ($00 instead) and a length field of 923: USR is evaluated before the ROM looks for the statement's end, and the code never returns, so neither is ever read. The header says 384 bytes with a program of 52: the other 332 bytes are the program's "variables" (VARS = $5CFF), six unused bytes, the code at $5D05 and the first stage.

$5CCB b BasicLine0 ; Line 0: RANDOMIZE USR (PEEK 23641+256*PEEK 23642+4), the hidden numbers 23635, 256, 23636, 58
> PEEK PROG + 256 x PEEK (PROG + 1) = `$5CCB`, the line's own address; + 58 = `$5D05`, the code after the program. Each number's digits are followed by `$0E` and the five-byte form the ROM uses; three of the four differ from the digits.
> c1: the ROM runs the line after the load and USR enters `$5D05` with BC = `$5D05`.
$5CCB ; the line number, 0 (high byte first)
$5CCD ; the line's length, 923: wrong, never read (the line never ends)
$5CCF ; RANDOMIZE ($F9) USR ($C0) "(" PEEK ($BE)
$5CD3 ; "23641", $0E and 23635 = $5C53 (PROG)
$5CDE ; "+" "256", $0E and 256
$5CE8 ; "*" PEEK "23642", $0E and 23636 = $5C54
$5CF5 ; "+" "4", $0E and 58
$5CFD ; ")", then $00 where ENTER would be
$5CFF b BasicGap ; Six bytes between the program and the code: VARS points here, nothing reads them
> The first bytes of the program's "variables" area. No code of the load reads them (c1).

$5D05 c BasicCode ; USR's code: copy the loader's first stage ($134 bytes from $5D17) to $B626 and run it
> Entered by USR with BC = `$5D05`, the routine's own address: HL = BC + `$12` = `$5D17`, wherever the program was loaded. SP = `$5C00`, the stack below the main block's load address (the stack stays there to the end of the load). LDIR `$134` bytes to `$B626-$B759` and `JP $B65B` (Loader1Start). Interrupts are still on: Loader1Start turns them off after clearing the screen.
> Witness: L1 1 (c1).
$5D08 ; HL = USR's address + $12: the first stage's bytes
@isub $5D09 LD DE,HookCount
@isub $5D14 JP Loader1Start

@module loader1 ; The loader's first stage at $B626: its variables, the pilot search, the turbo byte and block loads
@about Copied by BasicCode from $5D17 to $B626-$B759 and run from Loader1Start $B65B: clear the screen, copy the ROM font to $B326 (the loading screen's character set, which the fourth load partly overwrites), find the turbo block's pilot (FindPilot) and load stage 2's first piece ($10F bytes to $B75A) with LoadBlock, then JP $B856 in that piece. Everything later (the rest of stage 2, the glyphs, the script, the game) is loaded by stage 2 through LoadBlock.
@about The turbo encoding (work/ann/04-loader.md). The edge waits count 56 T passes in B; one bit is one pulse pair (two edges, EdgePair). A 0 is a pair of 836 T pulses, a 1 a pair of 1670 T: from B = $B0, a count above $CB is a 1. ReadByte reads 9 pairs, 8 bits MSB first and an invert flag (the byte is the complement when the flag is 1, so at most 4 of the 8 bits are ones), then as many padding pairs as the zeros less 5. A 1 lasts as long as two 0s, so every byte takes the time of 13 short pairs (21720-21736 T, about 6.2 ms) whatever its value.
@about LoadBlock calls ByteHook after every byte. It keeps the checksum H (the XOR of the block's bytes, plus `$FF` for every hook) and counts HookCount down. LoadBlock has no RET: after the checksum byte it falls into ByteHook, so that byte is counted too and H XOR L leaves H = 0 for the next load; once stage 2 has armed the counter, every 8th count runs stage 2's work at $B75A (the countdown and the loading screen's script), which reads its own pulse pairs from the tape, 7 of them, the time of one more byte. So the tape and the code stay in step without any pause in the block.
@about No border stripes and no BREAK test: the edge loop samples EAR (bit 6 of port $FE) in a 56 T loop of the ROM's length, with a never-taken RET C and a NOP where the ROM tests SPACE. A timeout or a bad checksum jumps to $0000: the machine resets.
$B626 b HookCount ; The loader's variables (read and written by stage 2 as well), then the copyright text
> HookCount `$B626`: ByteHook's down-counter. 0 until stage 2 sets it to 8 (`$B856`), so the hook does nothing during the first load.
> The countdown (`$B627-$B62A`), the flags (`$B62B`), the script's print state (`$B62C-$B633`), the countdown's place (`$B634-$B637`), the screen set-up (`$B638`, `$B639`), the script's window (`$B63B-$B640`), the character set (`$B641`) and the control codes' work bytes (`$B643-$B648`). Names and readers: work/ann/04-loader.md.
$B627 TickHooks ; hooks left to the countdown's next tick (5 at first, then 20: about 0.99 s)
$B628 Countdown ; the countdown, three ASCII digits, "294": the seconds left to the end of the load
$B62B LoadFlags ; bit 0 the countdown runs, bit 1 the script runs, bit 2 a control code repeats, bit 3 a picture
$B62C ScriptWait ; hooks to wait before the next script step ($03 n sets it; 1 = every hook)
$B62D PrintRow ; the print position's row: the attribute address of its column 0 ($5AC0: row 22)
$B62F PrintColumn ; the print position's column (0-31, absolute)
$B630 AttrPair ; read as a word: C (this byte, unused) and B = PrintAttr for PrintGlyph
$B631 PrintAttr ; the attribute of printed characters (7: white ink on black)
$B632 ScriptPtr ; the script's next byte: SerialText $B835 first, then $998E
$B634 CountdownAt ; the countdown's first digit: an attribute address ($5AC1: row 22, column 1)
$B636 CountdownPair ; read as a word: C (unused) and B = CountdownAttr
$B637 CountdownAttr ; the countdown's attribute, $57 (bright, white on red)
$B638 ClsAttr ; the attribute Loader1Start clears the screen to (0: black)
$B639 BorderColour ; the border FindPilot sets (0: black), with MIC
$B63A ; $20, not read by the tape's code
$B63B WindowLeft ; the script window's first column (0)
$B63C WindowRight ; the script window's last column (31)
$B63D WindowTop ; the window's top row: the attribute address of its column 0 ($5800)
$B63F WindowBottom ; the window's bottom row, the same way ($5AE0: row 23)
$B641 FontPtr ; the character set: the 8 bytes of character 32 ($B326, the ROM font's copy)
$B643 FillChar ; the character $0C, $0E and $1D print (32: space; $05 sets it)
$B644 RepeatCount ; $0E's count left; in a picture, the column within the row
$B645 PictureWidth ; a picture's cells a row ($0F's fourth byte)
$B646 PictureRows ; a picture's rows left ($0F's third byte)
$B647 PictureAt ; the attribute address of the picture row's first cell
$B649 t CopyrightText ; "(C) D.G.COOKE 1987": never read
> 18 bytes up to the first instruction. The code does not read them (the chain in the main block holds the same author's "COPYRIGHT D.G.COOKE 1987", src/head).

$B65B c Loader1Start ; The first stage: clear the screen, copy the ROM font to $B326, find the pilot, load stage 2's first piece
> From BasicCode's `JP $B65B`.
> 1. The attributes `$5800-$5AFF` filled with ClsAttr (0) and the pixels `$4000-$57FF` with 0, both by LDDR from the top (BC = 0 after the first LDDR gives the 0 for the second).
> 2. LDIR the 768 bytes of RomFont `$3D00` to FontPtr's `$B326`: characters 32-127 for PrintGlyph. The fourth load replaces characters 32-90 with Deflektor's own.
> 3. DI; FindPilot (no pilot within its time: `JP $0000`); H = 0, the checksum; LoadBlock IX = `$B75A`, DE = `$010F`, stage 2's first piece; `JP $B856` (LoadRest, in that piece).
> Witness: L1 2 (c1).
$B66A ; the pixels: the byte below the attributes, then LDDR $17FF more
$B672 ; the ROM font, 3 x 256 bytes (C = 0 after the LDDR)
$B67D ; interrupts off for the rest of the load
$B684 ; the checksum starts at 0 (each later load starts at 0 too: LoadBlock's end)
$B686 ; stage 2's first piece, $10F bytes to $B75A

$B693 c FindPilot ; Find the turbo block's pilot and its sync: NC when no edge comes within 2000 tries
> The border to BorderColour with MIC set (`OR $08`); C = the port `$FE` level now (the edge waits compare bit 6 with it).
> 1. Up to 2000 EdgeWait calls for a first edge; none: `RET` with NC (HL = 0).
> 2. Then pilot pairs (EdgePair from B = `$9C`) whose count must pass `$C6` (a pair of 2127 T pulses gives about 60): 256 - H in a row (H = the high byte of the tries left, so 249 in c1), any short pair starts again.
> 3. The sync: single edges from B = `$C9` while the count reaches `$D4` (the rest of the pilot); the first shorter one is the first sync pulse (693 T), and `JP $B70E` waits for the second's edge. Returns with carry set.
> The same plan as the ROM's LD-LEADER and LD-SYNC with its own constants. c1 counted 249 pilot pairs, then 4337 pilot edges to the sync.
> Witness: L1 2 (c1).
$B69D ; C = the EAR level now (bit 6 is compared)
$B6A2 FindPilotStart ; HL = 2000 tries for an edge
$B6A5 FindPilotEdge
$B6B0 FindPilotPairs ; the pilot: pairs above $C6 from $9C, 256 - H in a row
$B6B6 FindPilotPair
$B6C5 FindPilotSync ; edges from $C9 while they count to $D4: the sync is the first short one

$B6D4 c ReadByte ; Read one byte into L: 8 pairs MSB first and an invert flag, then the padding pairs; NC on a timeout
> DE is kept. D = `$FC`, E = 9 pairs. Each pair is EdgePair from the B the caller or the last pair left (B = `$B0` after each); a count above `$CB` is a 1. Each bit goes into L by the next pass's `RL L`, so after 9 passes L holds bits 1-8 and the carry the ninth, the invert flag: `SBC A,A; XOR L` complements L when the flag is 1. D counts the 0 pairs from -4.
> Then D - 1 = zeros - 5 padding pairs, never tested (FILL bits). So a byte is 9 + zeros - 5 pairs; with a 1 as long as two 0s, every byte lasts 13 short pairs. The encoder writes the form with fewer ones (tools/tapesim.py `byte_bits`), so zeros is 5 to 9 and the padding 0 to 4.
> Out: L = the byte, carry set; NC (and B wrapped) on a timeout. Called by LoadBlock for each byte and for the checksum.
> Witness: L1 82906 (c1).
$B6D5 ; D = -4 (zeros counted up), E = 9 pairs
$B6D8 ReadByteBit ; the last pair's bit into L
$B6DF ; a count above $CB is a 1
$B6E4 ; a 0: one more zero
$B6E5 ReadByteNext
$B6EA ; the ninth bit, the invert flag, complements L
$B6ED ; B + 1: the code's time between this pair and the next
$B6F1 ReadBytePad ; a padding pair, its value not tested
$B6F8 ReadBytePadCount ; D - 1 more: zeros - 5 padding pairs
$B6FB ReadByteEnd

$B6FD c PadPairs ; Stage 2's hook padding: read pairs until L's marker bit leaves bit 7, then L = $FF
> Called by stage 2's HookWork (`$B784`). L holds a marker bit (2 at the hook's start) that every pair the hook reads moves up: here each pair shifts a 1 in with `RL L` until the marker falls out into the carry. So the hook always reads 7 pairs in all (the countdown's and the script's included), 6 long and the last short (tools/tapesim.py gives 0 when bit 7 of L is set): the time of 13 short pairs, one byte's.
> Out: L = `$FF` and carry set (stage 2 then gives that L to ByteHook as if it were a byte: XORed into H and counted); NC on a timeout.
> Witness: L1 60788 (c1).

$B70A c EdgePair ; Wait for two edges (one pulse pair), counting in B; NC on a timeout
> EdgeWait twice (the second by falling into it). Called by FindPilot, ReadByte, PadPairs, LoadBlock and stage 2.
> Witness: L1 914788 (c1).
$B70E c EdgeWait ; Wait 350 T, then for the next edge, counting 56 T passes in B: carry = an edge, NC = B wrapped
> A delay of 25 x 14 T, then EdgeNoDelay `$B714` (stage 2 calls it directly): `AND A` (carry clear) and the loop. A pass: `INC B` (a wrap to 0 is the timeout: `RET Z` with NC), port `$FE` read with A = 0, XOR C, `AND $40` (EAR). An edge: C = NOT C (the new level), carry set.
> The pass is the ROM's LD-SAMPLE length (56 T); a never-taken `RET C` and a `NOP` stand where the ROM tests SPACE and changes the border, so the screen keeps its border and BREAK does nothing.
> Witness: L1 1857012 (c1).
$B710 EdgeDelay
$B714 EdgeNoDelay ; the entry without the delay (stage 2's script and countdown pairs)
$B715 EdgeLoop
$B722 ; an edge: the new level in C, carry set

$B727 c LoadBlock ; Load DE bytes to IX: one lead pair, the bytes (ByteHook after each), the checksum byte against H, then on into ByteHook
> IX = the address, DE = the length; H = the running checksum. A lead pair (EdgePair from B = `$B0`; NC: `JP $0000`). For each byte: ReadByte (NC: reset), store L at (IX), IX + 1, B + 3 (the time of this code before the next pair), ByteHook. Then ReadByte for the checksum byte and `JP NZ,$0000` unless it equals H. There is no RET: the code runs on into ByteHook, which XORs the checksum byte into H (H = 0, the next load's start), counts it in HookCount like a data byte (so the hooks keep their period across the loads) and returns to LoadBlock's caller. IX is left past the block.
> Called 8 times: by Loader1Start (`$B75A`, `$10F`) and by stage 2 (LoadRest: `$B869`, `$74` and `$B8DD`, `$302`; LoadFiles: `$B326`, `$01D8`; `$998E`, `$1998`; `$C000`, `$4000`; `$5C00`, `$4000`; `$9C00`, `$01F0`).
> Witness: L1 16 (c1).
$B72F ; the first byte's pair counts from $B0
$B731 LoadBlockByte
$B737 ; store the byte (tools/tapesim.py reads the loads here)
$B73C ; B + 3: the store's time
$B747 ; the checksum byte: equal to H, the XOR of the block's bytes (and $FF a hook)
$B74D ; L = H, else reset; then on into ByteHook (H = 0)

$B752 c ByteHook ; After each byte: H = H XOR L; once stage 2 has armed it, every 8th call runs stage 2's work
> The checksum first. Then A = HookCount - 1: negative (HookCount 0, before stage 2 arms it) returns at once (`RET M`). Otherwise the routine runs on past this stage's end into Stage2 `$B75A`, which stores the count and returns until it is 0, then does the countdown and the script, reads the hook's pairs, and comes back here with L = `$FF` (counted and XORed like a byte; work/ann/04-loader.md).
> Called by LoadBlock after each byte, entered by LoadBlock's end after the checksum byte, and jumped to by stage 2 at the end of its work (L = `$FF`).
> c1: 5882 hooks ran (tools/tapesim.py run, work/ann/04-loader/hooks.py): after 8 bytes of the second load, then every 7 bytes and the hook's own count (6 data bytes and the last load's checksum byte at the start of the third to eighth loads).
> Witness: L1 94670 (c1).
$B758 ; HookCount 0 (not armed): return; else on into stage 2 at $B75A
